## 1. Publish the Protean jar to mavenLocal (SNAPSHOT / not-yet-on-Central only)

From the **protean repo**, once (and again whenever the library changes):

```bash
cd <protean repo>
./gradlew publishToMavenLocal
```

If the coordinate is a released version already on Maven Central, skip this step.

## 2. Start the database (data-access samples only)

```bash
docker compose up -d
```

Override the target with `DB_HOST` / `DB_PORT` / `DB_USER` / `DB_PASSWORD` (see `application.yml`).

## 3. Run the server

```bash
./gradlew run
```

Override the HTTP port with `SERVER_PORT` (it drives both `server.port` and the advertised MCP address, so the two
cannot drift). `./setup.sh` uses the same variable.

## 4. Deploy a module over MCP (when MCP is enabled)

> **MCP is an RCE surface** — it compiles and hot-loads submitted sources. This sample enables it with
> permissive authorization for a **local demo only**. For anything real, add authentication (Bearer/OAuth via
> Spring Security) and a `ModuleActionAuthorizer` policy — see [§Security](#security).

List the tools:

```bash
curl -s localhost:8080/platform/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"tools/list"}'
```

Deploy a module (`deploy_module` carries sources in `files[]`; FQCN = package + filename). Over the raw JSON-RPC
endpoint the tool name uses a **dot** (`protean.deploy_module`), not the underscore of the MCP client tool.

```bash
curl -s localhost:8080/platform/mcp -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{
  "jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/call","params":{
    "name":"protean.deploy_module",
    "arguments":{
      "id":"hello","version":"1","controller":"gen.HelloController",
      "files":[
        {"kind":"source","filename":"HelloController.java","content":"package gen;\nimport org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;\n@RestController\npublic class HelloController {\n  public static String up(String s){ return s.toUpperCase(); }\n  @GetMapping(\"/hello\")\n  public String hello(@RequestParam(defaultValue=\"world\") String name){ return \"hello \"+up(name); }\n}\n"},
        {"kind":"test","filename":"HelloTest.java","content":"package gen;\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;\nimport static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertEquals;\nclass HelloTest {\n  @Test void up(){ assertEquals(\"HI\", HelloController.up(\"hi\")); }\n}\n"}
      ]
    }
  }}'
```

**Streaming and WebSocket.** A module may return an `SseEmitter` — nothing here restricts it to request/response,
and `protean.module.request-timeout-ms` does not cut an async stream short. Prefer `isolation.mode=in-process` for
it: under worker/container the reverse proxy relays the body without flushing, so small events can sit in the
buffer, and never declare a verify probe on a streaming path (the probe has no read timeout and would hang the
deploy). **WebSocket/STOMP cannot be a module route** — protean registers module endpoints as request mappings, and
WebSocket handlers have no runtime-registration path; put a WS endpoint in this host app instead.

### Module authoring rules

**Extend the base types this app provides.** In the MVC shape (Controller → Service → Mapper) the Service and the
Mapper inherit from `src/main/java/{{PKG_PATH}}/support/`:

```java
public class OrdersService extends {{PKG}}.support.BaseService { ... }

@Mapper
public interface OrdersMapper extends {{PKG}}.support.BaseMapper { ... }
```

Both are empty. They exist so a cross-cutting concern can be added in one file later and reach every module,
instead of requiring an edit to each module that was already deployed. The Controller extends nothing — its seam
is `CommonInterceptor`, which already sees module routes.

**`@Transactional` goes on the Service. Not the Mapper, not the Controller.** A MyBatis Mapper is a proxy
generated by `MapperFactoryBean`, so the annotation there is not honoured — it looks like a boundary and is not
one. On a Controller the transaction would wrap request parsing and response serialisation, holding the connection
for the whole exchange.

> **`@Transactional` is silently ignored until your module enables it.** A module runs in a child
> `ApplicationContext`; `@EnableTransactionManagement` and Boot's transaction autoconfiguration ran in the *host*
> context, and Spring's BeanPostProcessors are per-context — so the host's transaction advisor never proxies a
> module bean. The method just runs, without a transaction and without an error. Either add
> `@EnableTransactionManagement` plus a `PlatformTransactionManager` to your module's own `@Configuration`, or
> inject the host's `PlatformTransactionManager` and use a `TransactionTemplate` (nothing to forget, nothing to
> no-op).
>
> Scope of the transaction once it works: in-process with this app's `DataSource` and transaction manager →
> participates in the host transaction. In-process with a `DataSource` the module creates → independent. Under
> `worker`/`container` isolation → a separate process, so always independent.

The bundled test runs as a **deploy gate**; it must pass for the module to reach `ACTIVE`. Then:

```bash
curl 'localhost:8080/hello?name=protean'      # → hello PROTEAN
curl localhost:8080/platform/modules          # deployment state
```

